{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>But if a day or two days.</b> Rashi explained: it is included in [the general category of] 'one who strikes a man and he dies,' but the verse came and removed it from the general category to be judged by the law of a day or two. And if you ask: why not say that this was included and went out from the general category — 'it went out not to teach about itself but to teach about the entire general category,' so we would say that even an Israelite is judged by the law of a day or two? One may answer that, on the contrary, something that was included in a general category and went out to be judged by a new matter — this is a case where you cannot return it to its general category. And if you ask: this works out well for the one who says that something that was included in a general category and went out to be judged by a new matter — it does not derive from its general category and its general category does not derive from it; but for the one who says its general category does derive from it, what can be said? From this we would derive that an Israelite is judged by a day or two! One may answer: regarding these [cases] that derive from other matters, its general category does derive from it; nevertheless, regarding the new matter, everyone agrees that even its general category does not derive from it. And if you ask: for the one who says it does not derive from its general category and its general category does not derive from it — regarding other matters, whence do we know that a murderer who killed an Israelite with a sword [is liable, for] ״he shall surely be avenged״ — here it is written regarding a slave, so we do not derive! One may answer: it is derived from ״שופך דם האדם באדם דמו ישפך״, and the shedding of blood is only with a sword. Or alternatively, from ״ואתה תבער״, as is concluded in Ketubot, chapter אלו נערות. But if this is difficult, this is difficult: how can one derive that with a staff — i.e., one that has enough to kill — for an Israelite, as we explained? And everyone agrees that it [the slave case] does not derive from its general category! One may answer: by force of an a fortiori argument it is at any rate derived. And therefore Rashi adopted this language: 'and is it not an a fortiori matter' — and if an Israelite, whose [liability is] stringent, is not liable except with something that has enough to kill, a slave, who is lenient — is it not all the more so! And it is stated in the Mekhilta: 'If you say thus, you are punishing him from the law.' But what does 'a staff' mean? On the contrary, from this a fortiori argument we arrive at leniency for him. One may answer: you can find stringency in a pursuer — for it is established for us that a pursuer who pursues his fellow to kill him, it is permitted to save [the pursued] with the life of the pursuer. And this master was pursuing his slave to kill him with a staff that does not have enough to kill, and another Israelite came and killed the master to save the slave. And since it is derived from this law that the master is not liable because it [the staff] does not have enough to kill, it turns out that this Israelite who killed the master is liable to death — so you find that you make him liable and punish him from the law. From Rabbi Yitzchak of Pitobon, of blessed memory."], :book :exodus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The final attribution 'מהר\"י יצחק מפיטוב\"ן ז\"ל' is rendered as 'Rabbi Yitzchak of Pitobon, of blessed memory.' The manuscript likely reads מהר\"ר or similar; the exact acronym prefix is uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:03:53.979764337Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}